{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_017ydCokZjy8FyAvsV1JoWzG", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Both files loaded. Now evaluating the skill against all 14 patterns.\n\n### Steps Executed\n- Step 1: Input Handling \u2014 completed\n- Step 2: Load Knowledge \u2014 completed\n- Step 3: Baseline Evaluation \u2014 completed\n- Step 4: Already-Optimal Detection \u2014 not optimal, continued\n- Step 5: Enhancement Targeting \u2014 completed, 5 targets selected\n- Step 6: Rewrite \u2014 completed\n- Step 7: File Splitting \u2014 skipped (under 500 lines)\n- Step 8: Prompt Plugin Delegation \u2014 skipped (plugin not available)\n- Step 9: Changelog \u2014 in progress\n\n## Enhanced Skill\n\n```markdown\n---\nname: \"pr:review\"\ndescription: \"Review a pull request for code quality, test coverage, and architectural alignment. Use when asked to review a PR, check code quality, provide feedback on changes, or audit a diff. Do NOT use for creating PRs (use pr:create) or for merging (use pr:merge).\"\nargument-hint: \"<pr-number> [--focus security|performance|correctness] [--brief]\"\nallowed-tools: [\"Read\", \"Bash\", \"Glob\"]\nuser-invocable: true\n---\n\n# Pull Request Review\n\nPerform a structured code review on a GitHub pull request, covering correctness, test coverage, security, performance, and architectural fit.\n\n## Why This Skill Exists\n\nCode reviews catch bugs before they reach production, but review quality varies wildly. Junior reviewers miss security issues; senior reviewers skip test coverage. This skill applies a consistent checklist so nothing falls through the cracks, while leaving subjective design decisions to the human reviewer.\n\n## Procedure\n\n### Step 1: Fetch the Diff\n\n```bash\ngh pr diff ${pr_number}\n```\n\nRead the diff. If `--brief` is set, summarize the changes in 3-5 sentences and skip to Step 5.\n\n**Gotcha**: Large diffs (>2000 lines) should be reviewed file-by-file rather than as a monolithic diff, because reviewers lose track of cross-file state beyond that size. If the diff exceeds this threshold, switch to per-file review mode automatically and note this in the output.\n\n**Completion check**: You have the full diff loaded (or have switched to per-file mode for large diffs). If the PR number is not found or the diff is empty, stop with the corresponding error from the Error Handling table.\n\n### Step 2: Check for Common Issues\n\nReview each changed file for the categories below. Start with correctness, because logic errors cause the most user-facing damage. Then check security and performance, prioritizing whichever matches `--focus`.\n\n**Gotcha**: Do not review generated files (lockfiles, compiled output, vendored dependencies). These inflate finding counts without actionable value. If a file looks generated, skip it and note the skip.\n\n**Correctness**:\n- Off-by-one errors in loops and slices\n- Nil/null pointer dereference risks\n- Resource leaks (unclosed files, connections, channels)\n- Race conditions in concurrent code\n\n**Security** (prioritize if `--focus security`):\n- SQL injection via string concatenation\n- Unsanitized user input in templates or commands\n- Hardcoded secrets or credentials\n- Missing authentication/authorization checks\n\n**Performance** (prioritize if `--focus performance`):\n- N+1 query patterns\n- Unbounded collection growth\n- Missing pagination on list endpoints\n- Expensive operations inside loops\n\nUse these severity levels when recording findings:\n\n| Severity | Criteria |\n|----------|----------|\n| critical | Causes data loss, security vulnerability, or crash in production |\n| warning | Incorrect behavior or degraded performance that affects users |\n| info | Style, naming, or minor improvements that do not affect correctness |\n\n**Gotcha**: Resist reporting style issues as warnings. A missing blank line is not a warning; an unhandled error path is. Inflated severities erode reviewer trust.\n\n**Completion check**: Every changed file (except generated files) has been reviewed against all applicable categories.\n\n### Step 3: Verify Test Coverage\n\nThis step requires output from Step 2 (you need to know what changed to assess whether tests cover it).\n\nCheck if the PR includes tests for:\n- New public functions/methods\n- Changed behavior (not just changed code)\n- Error paths and edge cases\n\n```bash\ngh pr checks ${pr_number}\n```\n\nNote any failing CI checks. Test coverage matters separately from correctness because even correct code breaks later without regression tests.\n\n**Completion check**: You have assessed test adequacy for every behavioral change found in Step 2, and CI check status is recorded.\n\n### Step 4: Assess Architectural Fit\n\nThis step requires output from Step 2 (the changed files determine which patterns to compare against).\n\nCompare the changes against:\n- Existing patterns in the same package/module (read 2-3 neighboring files for comparison, not the entire directory)\n- The project's documented architecture (if available in `docs/` or `ARCHITECTURE.md`)\n- Separation of concerns (is business logic leaking into handlers?)\n\n**Completion check**: Architectural findings, if any, are recorded with file references.\n\n### Step 5: Generate Review\n\nOutput a structured review using this template:\n\n```markdown\n## PR #${pr_number} Review\n\n### Summary\n<2-3 sentence summary of what the PR does>\n\n### Findings\n\n| Severity | File | Line | Issue |\n|----------|------|------|-------|\n| ...      | ...  | ...  | ...   |\n\n### Test Coverage\n<Assessment of test adequacy>\n\n### Recommendation\n<APPROVE | REQUEST_CHANGES | COMMENT>\n<Brief justification>\n```\n\n**Filled example**:\n\n```markdown\n## PR #142 Review\n\n### Summary\nAdds rate limiting to the `/api/tokens` endpoint using a sliding window counter backed by Redis. Also refactors the middleware chain to support per-route configuration.\n\n### Findings\n\n| Severity | File | Line | Issue |\n|----------|------|------|-------|\n| critical | api/tokens.go | 87 | Redis connection is not checked for nil before use; will panic if Redis is unavailable |\n| warning | middleware/rate.go | 34 | Window size is hardcoded to 60s; should read from config to match other rate limiters |\n| info | api/tokens_test.go | 12 | Test name `TestIt` is not descriptive; consider `TestRateLimitRejectsExcessRequests` |\n\n### Test Coverage\nHappy path is covered. Missing: test for Redis connection failure, test for window boundary behavior.\n\n### Recommendation\nREQUEST_CHANGES\nThe nil Redis connection panic (critical) must be fixed before merge. The hardcoded window size is a secondary concern.\n```\n\nWhen no findings are detected, leave the Findings table empty and recommend APPROVE. When `--brief` is set, the output is just the Summary section (2-3 sentences) without the full review structure.\n\nThe table format makes it easy to address findings one by one. The recommendation gives a clear signal. The summary helps other reviewers who arrive late.\n\n## Error Handling\n\n| Condition | Action |\n|-----------|--------|\n| PR number not found | Stop: \"PR #${pr_number} not found. Check the number and repo.\" |\n| No diff available | Stop: \"PR has no changes or is already merged.\" |\n| CI checks still running | Note in review: \"CI checks in progress, results may change.\" |\n| Diff too large (>2000 lines) | Switch to per-file mode, note in output |\n\n## Known Limitations\n\n- Cannot assess runtime behavior or performance regressions (use profiling tools for that)\n- Architecture assessment depends on documented patterns; undocumented conventions may be missed\n- Security review is pattern-based, not a substitute for SAST tools like Semgrep or CodeQL\n```\n\n---\n\n## What Changed\n\n| Pattern Applied | What was added/changed | Why |\n|----------------|----------------------|-----|\n| Explain-the-Why | Added reasoning for the 2000-line threshold (\"reviewers lose track of cross-file state\"), why correctness is checked first (\"logic errors cause the most user-facing damage\"), why test coverage is a separate step (\"even correct code breaks later without regression tests\") | Several instructions were bare imperatives without rationale, making edge-case judgment harder |\n| Template Scaffold | Added a filled example of the review template showing a realistic PR #142 with critical/warning/info findings and a REQUEST_CHANGES recommendation | The output template had placeholders but no demonstration of what a completed review looks like, leaving severity classification ambiguous |\n| In-Skill Examples | Added a severity criteria table (critical/warning/info with definitions), the filled review example, and edge-case notes for no-findings and `--brief` outputs | Severity assignment was undefined, so the skill relied on Claude's default judgment rather than calibrated criteria |\n| Known Gotchas | Added two inline gotchas: skip generated files in Step 2, and resist inflating severity levels in Step 2. Enhanced the existing large-diff gotcha with reasoning | Code review has well-known pitfalls beyond large diffs; reviewing vendored code and over-reporting style issues as warnings are common mistakes |\n| Execution Checklist | Added completion checks after Steps 1-4, and explicit dependency declarations for Steps 3 and 4 (\"requires output from Step 2\") | Steps lacked completion criteria and dependencies were implicit, making it possible to skip or misordered steps |\n\n## Status Change\n\n| Pattern | Before | After |\n|---------|--------|-------|\n| Explain-the-Why | present | strong |\n| Template Scaffold | present | strong |\n| In-Skill Examples | present | strong |\n| Known Gotchas | present | strong |\n| Execution Checklist | present | strong |\n\n**Summary**: 5 patterns improved, 0 new files proposed\n\n---"}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 5751, "cache_read_input_tokens": 45687, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 5751, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "29cf9b9e-581f-4b78-880b-890a6733d6d9", "uuid": "3b3bdfdc-0eb5-4333-b244-dcbffecfb657", "request_id": "req_vrtx_011CbccRHbGB2rbGTRNqvJ7i", "timestamp": "2026-06-01T13:25:53.191Z"}
